✓ COMPOSED HEADLINE — full engine, LongMemEval-M full-haystack
  This number is COMPOSED from two ledgers, not measured in one 48q run:
    30/40  carried unchanged from the baseline ledger (5 categories NEVER re-run with the engine)
     5/8   measured with the engine (multi-session only)
    35/48  = 72.9%   recomputed
          claimed: 35/48 = 72.9%  → ok
  Because the 40 carried questions were never retried with the engine, they can only
  improve on a full re-run — which is why 72.9% is published as a LOWER BOUND, and
  why it must not be quoted as a measured 48-question engine result.

✓ LEXICAL CHANNEL — paired strict delta (answer side, development sample)
  recomputed from the rows' baseline_correct field: +9 gained / −1 regressed (claimed +9/−1)
  29/48 → 37/48 under the both-runs replay rule; exact binomial on 9-vs-1 flips gives p = 0.0215.
  ⚠ This 48-question sample was used during development and runs ~13 points easier than
    its parent set. The transfer evidence is the deterministic holdout below, not this pair.

✓ LEXICAL CHANNEL — the same runs under the FLEXIBLE judge (July's grader)
  34/48 = 70.8% → 39/48 = 81.3%, same both-runs replay rule
  paired: +7 gained / −2 regressed — a weaker signal than the strict pair above.
  2 question(s) disagreed between the two runs and were scored by the conjunction.
  Both leniencies are published because a score without its judge is not a result:
  the strict number is the one we lead with, the flexible one is what July would have said.
✓ LEXICAL CHANNEL — deterministic retrieval (no LLM in the loop)
  development sample: evidence sessions 38/48 → 41/48 · answer chunk 25/35 → 30/35 (matches the published 38→41 and 25→30)
  storage equivalence: persistent index reproduces the in-memory ranking on all 48 questions → ok
  app-isolation fix: CORE-mode ranking unchanged on all 48 questions → ok
  HOLDOUT (48 questions never seen during development): sessions +4/−0, answer chunks +2/−0 → zero regressions, as published

✓ ALL LEDGERS CONSISTENT — every published headline, including the composed one, is the exact sum of the published rows.
